branches.task:
METHODOLOGY REVIEW (round {args.round}).

Review this proposed CANDIDATE-GENERATOR design (the 'explore' half of a daily AI-response optimization loop; the 'measure' half — a locked 3-layer eval — scores candidates):

{args.proposal}

Prior-round critique to check is addressed:
{args.prior_critique}

Judge ONLY through an ML/experiment-methodology lens: (1) Is the generate -> score-pairwise-vs-baseline -> rank -> emit-top-5 loop sound and does it actually produce improvement signal, not noise? (2) Is the train/holdout split of failing cases a REAL anti-overfitting guard, or is it leaky (same author, same domain, tiny N, class imbalance)? Is it applied correctly? (3) Is the ranking metric (net = target-wins*severity - regressions - cost) well-formed and non-gameable? Does it double-count or ignore variance/significance? (4) Is failure-cluster the right generation unit vs per-case? (5) Attribution: can we actually claim a candidate caused the delta? For EACH design element output AGREE or OBJECT with a concrete fix. Cite experiment-design principles (holdout hygiene, multiple-comparisons, effect size vs p-hacking).

IRIS-DOMAIN REVIEW (round {args.round}).

Review this proposed CANDIDATE-GENERATOR design for the IRIS analyst-agent optimization loop:

{args.proposal}

Prior-round critique to check is addressed:
{args.prior_critique}

Judge ONLY through the lens of REAL IRIS improvement levers. Ground it in the worked example (an L2a:attribution failure cluster — IRIS reports a total when a grade-band segment was asked — fixed by a habit that says name the segment on every reported number). (1) Are the candidate TYPES (prompt/instruction edits, reflections/habits, tool schema/description edits) the right and sufficient levers for real IRIS failure modes, or are big levers missing (retrieval/context selection, SQL-generation prompting, lane routing)? (2) Is clustering by (failure-tag + domain) the right unit for IRIS, and will it produce coherent, actionable hypotheses? (3) Will proposed changes to shared prompts/habits realistically help one cluster without breaking other query types? (4) Is excluding eval-fixture edits from the generator correct, or does IRIS need fixture fixes routed somewhere concrete? Output AGREE or OBJECT per design element with a concrete fix.

SKEPTIC / RISK REVIEW (round {args.round}).

Stress-test this proposed CANDIDATE-GENERATOR design:

{args.proposal}

Prior-round critique to check is addressed:
{args.prior_critique}

Adversarial lens only: (1) REWARD HACKING — how can the generator raise the eval score without real quality gains (exploiting judge blind spots, editing prompts to pattern-match fixtures, gaming the affirmative/omission rules, targeting the advisory L3 dims that don't gate)? Does the train/holdout split actually stop this or just move it? (2) META CONFLICT OF INTEREST — the generator proposes changes to the same agent stack that the eval measures; is the eval-fixture exclusion enough of a firewall, or can it still contaminate baseline/holdout? (3) REGRESSION / BLAST RADIUS — a shared prompt/habit edit that fixes 4 cases and silently degrades 40 others; is the regression gate sufficient and does 'previously-passing' cover advisory L3 drops? (4) COST / RUNAWAY — eval-scoring every candidate across the whole suite each run; blowup and mitigation. (5) Does human-approval-before-merge create a rubber-stamp or a real gate? Output AGREE or OBJECT per element with the concrete failure mode + fix.

phases.task:
Three specialists reviewed the proposed candidate-generator design (round {args.round}).

PROPOSAL UNDER REVIEW:
{args.proposal}

SPECIALIST REVIEWS:
{steps.panel.output}

Decide consensus. Rules: consensus=true ONLY if NO specialist raised a material/blocking objection (minor wording nits are fine). Fold every ACCEPTED fix into revised_selections; reject fixes that conflict with the locked eval (DL-0022), DL-0020's top-5 cap, or DL-0021's portable-loop framing, and say why in critique. Keep it v1-scoped. Output ONLY JSON:
{
 "consensus": true|false,
 "revised_selections": "<the full, current best candidate-generator design incorporating accepted fixes>",
 "changes_from_proposal": "<what changed vs the proposal, or 'none'>",
 "critique": "<remaining open objections that block consensus, or 'none'>"
}
